  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "A finished action before a noun",
     "texto": [
      "In verbs, **-(으)ㄴ** before a noun usually presents the action as finished: 보다 → **본 영화** (the film I saw), 먹다 → **먹은 음식** (the food I ate), 만나다 → **만난 친구** (the friend I met). The form of the modifying clause does not by itself fix the tense of the whole sentence; context makes it clear."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "How it is formed",
     "texto": [
      "With verb stems ending in a vowel, add **-ㄴ**; with another consonant, **-은**: 가다 → **간 곳**, 먹다 → **먹은 음식**. Stems ending in **ㄹ** lose the ㄹ: 살다 → **산 집**, 만들다 → **만든 음식**. Adjectives, on the other hand, use -(으)ㄴ to describe a state: **큰 집** = big house. Contrast **산 집** (the house someone lived in / bought, depending on the verb and context) with **사는 집** (the house in which they live)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
